    basic: "Choose your Suit contracts early if you have a strong 6-card suit. Save the Nullo for when you have a hand full of 3s, 4s, and 5s. Never 'waste' a suit contract on a hand without at least one King or Ace. In Nullo, remember the Ace is your friendâ€”it's the lowest card (below the 3).",
    intermediate: "Manni Gambling: If your hand is 'almost' perfect for a Grand (e.g., 10 high cards), exchange your four lowest cards with the Manni. Even if you get worse cards, the risk is often worth it to secure a large point lead. Track which contracts your opponents have leftâ€”they may be forced into unfavorable situations.",
    advanced: "Defensive Teaming: Since it is 3 players, if the declarer chose 'Grand', the other two MUST cooperate to prevent them reaching 8 tricks. Lead suits that your partner is likely void in to force the declarer to use their high cards early. In Nullo, the declarer's target is only 4 tricksâ€”defenders should try to 'force' the declarer to win tricks by leading suits they must follow.",
    tips: [
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: "In Nullo, the Ace is the best card because it is 'unbeatable' (it's the lowest). Save your Aces for the end of the round when you might be forced to win.",
      },
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: "Keep track of which of the 18 rounds are left. If you still have a 'Nullo' left on round 17, and your hand is high, you are in serious trouble.",
      },
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: "Don't be greedy in the Manni exchange. Sometimes keeping a mediocre hand is better than risking a complete disaster.",
